course: biology
lesson: 68
title: The brain and nerves
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Your brain weighs about as much as a big bag of sugar, is soft like tofu, and runs everything you do, think, feel and remember.
explain: It talks to the body through nerves, long thin wires of living cells; the thick main cable, the spinal cord, runs down inside the bones of your back.
explain: Stub your toe and there is a strange gap: first a bump, then a moment later the ache arrives and you yell, because the signal has to travel all the way up your leg and spine to the brain, and pain signals are not the fastest ones.
explain: Some jobs are too urgent for the brain: when the doctor taps below your knee, the signal goes to the spinal cord and straight back to the leg, which kicks before you have even thought about it.
explain: The brain never switches off: while you sleep it sorts the day's memories and makes dreams, and every time you practise your times tables the paths between its cells grow stronger.
fact: The adult brain weighs about 1.3 to 1.4 kg and is soft like tofu; it is protected by the skull and floats in a clear fluid that cushions it.
fact: The brain has about 86 billion nerve cells; it is only about 2 percent of the body's weight but uses about a fifth of its energy.
fact: A helmet's foam crushes when it hits something, slowing the head down more gently so the brain is not knocked so hard against the skull.
fact: Nerves carry signals between the body and the brain; the fastest signals travel at over 100 metres a second, but some pain signals travel only about 1 or 2 metres a second.
fact: A reflex is a quick automatic movement: in the knee-jerk test the signal goes to the spinal cord and straight back to the muscle without waiting for the brain.
fact: The brain has two halves; the left half mostly controls the right side of the body and the right half controls the left side.
fact: The idea that people are «left-brained» or «right-brained», and that we use only 10 percent of our brain, are myths: we use all of it, though not all parts at once.
fact: During sleep the brain is busy: it sorts and stores the day's memories, clears away waste and helps the body grow and repair.
fact: Most vivid dreams happen in REM sleep, when the eyes dart about under the eyelids; everyone dreams several times a night, even if they forget.
fact: Each time you practise something, the connections between the nerve cells involved get stronger, so the memory gets easier to find.
fact: Brain freeze happens when very cold food chills the roof of the mouth quickly; blood vessels there change size and nerves send a pain signal felt in the forehead; slowing down and warming the roof of the mouth with your tongue makes it pass soon.
fact: The spinal cord is a thick bundle of nerves running through a tunnel in the bones of the spine, carrying messages between the brain and the body.
fact: An octopus has about 500 million nerve cells, and about two thirds of them are in its eight arms, which can taste and feel and act partly on their own.
fact: Pins and needles come when pressing on a nerve stops it sending signals properly; when the pressure is gone the nerve wakes up with lots of tingly signals.
exercise: About how much does an adult brain weigh? | About 1.3 to 1.4 kg | About 100 grams, like an apple | About 10 kg, like a big dog | About as much as a car tyre
exercise: What protects the soft brain? | The skull and a clear cushioning fluid | A thick layer of hard muscle all round it | Only the thick hair growing on the head | A bendy shell of cartilage like the nose
exercise: How does a bike helmet protect the brain? | Its foam crushes and slows the head more gently | It makes the head bounce away from the road like a rubber ball | It stops the brain from thinking during a fall | It keeps the brain warm on cold rides
exercise: You stub your toe and yell a moment later. Why the delay? | The pain signal travels up the leg and spine to the brain | The toe waits a moment to see if it is really hurt before telling anyone | The brain is always asleep for a second | Sound travels slowly out of your mouth
exercise: Why does your leg kick when the doctor taps below your knee? | A reflex: the signal goes to the spinal cord and straight back | The doctor's little hammer pushes the whole leg up into the air | You decide to kick to help the doctor | The knee has its own small brain inside
exercise: Which half of the brain mostly controls your right hand? | The left half | The right half | Only the back of the brain | Neither, the hand controls itself
exercise: How much of the brain do we really use? | All of it, though not every part at once | Only 10 percent, the rest is kept spare | Only the left half on school days | Only the front half
exercise: What does the brain do while you sleep? | It sorts the day's memories and clears away waste | It switches off completely until the alarm goes in the morning | It shrinks a little to rest | It sends all the blood down to the feet
exercise: When do most vivid dreams happen? | In REM sleep, when the eyes dart about | Only just before you wake up for school | Only when you sleep in the daytime after lunch | Never, dreams are only made up after we wake
exercise: Why does practising your times tables help you remember them? | Connections between nerve cells grow stronger | The numbers slowly stick to the inside of the skull | Your brain grows a new times-table part | Your eyes store the numbers for later
exercise: What causes brain freeze? | Very cold food chilling the roof of the mouth quickly | Ice crystals forming inside the brain itself for a moment | The whole brain getting frozen solid for a few seconds | Tiny bits of cold food going up the back into your nose
exercise: Where is your spinal cord? | Inside a tunnel through the bones of your back | Along the front of your tummy under the skin | Inside your arm and leg bones | Just under the skin of your chest
exercise: What does the spinal cord do? | Carries messages between the brain and the body | Pumps blood up from the heart to the brain all day | Holds food on its way to the stomach | Makes the voice deep
exercise: What is special about an octopus's nerve cells? | About two thirds of them are in its arms | It has no nerve cells at all, only muscles | They are all packed into one tiny round brain | They are all tucked away inside its big eyes
exercise: Why does your foot go to sleep after sitting cross-legged? | Pressure on a nerve stops it sending signals properly | The foot runs out of blood for good and has to grow more | The foot is tired and wants a nap | The bones in the foot slip out of place
exercise: About how much of the body's energy does the brain use? | About a fifth | Almost none | About half | All of it
test: lol my brother says he only uses 10% of his brain. what about the rest? | Myth: we use all of it, just not every part at once | True, the rest is spare and gets switched on when he is older | True, the rest is just water | True, the rest only works at night
test: In a cartoon the hero bumps his head and the brain inside rattles but nothing happens. Why do real people wear helmets? | A hard knock can bang the brain against the skull and hurt it | Brains cannot be hurt by bumps, helmets are only for show | Helmets are really only for keeping warm in winter | Helmets help you think faster on the road
test: What would happen if a nerve from the hand to the spinal cord were cut? | The brain would stop getting touch signals from that hand | The hand would slowly grow a little brain of its own to make up for it | The hand would feel everything twice as strongly | Nothing, hands do not need nerves
test: A girl's leg stays still when the doctor taps her knee, even though she is relaxed. What might the doctor think? | The reflex path through her spinal cord may not be working well | She has far too much brain power and it stops the kick | Her knee is too strong for the little hammer | Her leg has gone to sleep forever
test: Jake says left-brained people are good at maths and right-brained people at art. Right? | No, that is a myth; both halves work together in everyone | Yes, each person only ever uses one half of the brain at a time | Yes, and they can never change sides | No, artists use only their spinal cord
test: Why might a stubbed toe give a quick bump first and the real ache a moment later? | Different nerve signals travel at different speeds | The toe sends two letters to the brain by post, one after the other | The brain waits to decide if it cares | The ache has to go round the body twice
test: Ben slept badly before his spelling test and forgot words he knew. Why? | Sleep helps the brain sort and store memories | Sleep makes the brain smaller so words fit better | Words fall out of the ears at night | Spelling is stored in the stomach
test: argh ice cream headache. how do i stop it?? | Slow down and warm the roof of your mouth, it soon passes | Eat more ice cream even faster to freeze the headache out | Stand on your head till it goes | It lasts all day and nothing helps
test: An octopus at the aquarium opens a jar with its arms while its eyes look elsewhere. How can its arms be so clever? | Most of its nerve cells are in its arms, which can act partly on their own | Each arm has a full second brain just like ours, so it has eight extra brains | The octopus has tiny eyes on its arm tips | It is trained by a robot in the tank
test: In a film a robot says the brain is like a computer that is off while you sleep. What is wrong? | The brain stays busy in sleep, sorting memories and making dreams | Nothing is wrong, the brain really is switched off every night | The brain is only off at weekends | The brain is on only in REM sleep
test: Why would a doctor worry much more about a broken back than a broken arm? | The spinal cord inside the back carries messages to the whole body | The back is much heavier than the arm, so it is harder to lift | Backs can never heal once broken | The arm has no bones in it
test: You cross your legs for an hour and your foot goes numb, then tingles. What does the tingle mean? | The squashed nerve is waking up and sending signals again | The foot is freezing because the blood stopped for an hour | The blood has turned into fizz | The bone in the foot is growing
exam: Identify the pathway by which the knee-jerk reflex is effected. | Via the spinal cord directly to the muscle, without the brain | Via the brain, following a deliberate decision to move the leg | Via the bloodstream from the heart | Via the skin of the knee alone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the control of the body by the cerebral hemispheres? | Each hemisphere principally governs the opposite side of the body | Each hemisphere principally governs the same side of the body | The left hemisphere alone governs movement | The hemispheres govern no movement whatever
exam: It is asserted that human beings employ merely ten percent of the brain. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The assertion is false; all regions are employed, though not at once | The assertion is correct for adults but not for children | The assertion is correct during sleep alone | The assertion is correct for the left hemisphere
exam: Ascertain the principal means by which a protective helmet reduces injury to the brain upon impact. | Its foam deforms, decelerating the head more gradually | It renders the skull permanently thicker after each impact | It prevents the head from moving at all | It cools the brain upon impact
